{"Once upon a time there were four little Rabbits, and their names were— Flopsy, Mopsy, Cotton-tail, and Peter. They lived with their Mother in a sand-bank, underneath the root of a very big fir-tree.":"7dc0af89f15e75959a7af0deaec9c738bce744c99f2fb00378cee65c6bb7e748","“Now my dears,” said old Mrs. Rabbit one morning, “you may go into the fields or down the lane, but don't go into Mr. McGregor's garden: your Father had an accident there; he was put in a pie by Mrs. McGregor.”":"67b9c64e4202d718a324c4878821d55b6fba65a8a80a9155b1750d07162decde","“Now run along, and don't get into mischief. I am going out.”":"a15d330b6b617e4af0bd0719395880ede48577eef93f5e7936e3c39a6b5318d8","Then old Mrs. Rabbit took a basket and her umbrella, and went through the wood to the baker's. She bought a loaf of brown bread and five currant buns.":"8089a33399f42998fca84a9d972e499ccf11fe1f91202e5a3e93a92836899ad7","Flopsy, Mopsy, and Cotton-tail, who were good little bunnies, went down the lane to gather blackberries.":"15711db3cc782b6a76f6ad6a3543ee22c7a6f4f44662dc57306411c5c19cd43f","But Peter, who was very naughty, ran straight away to Mr. McGregor's garden, and squeezed under the gate!":"c6f40eb0e681c8ff07b78f928ab9b9938e81dcaba50b88a3f57976fa8a5f85e8","First he ate some lettuces and some French beans; and then he ate some radishes;":"d16c4e3fa40ba78c6a7991984524c92aab0fe99e84dbe354201164d9cc232e27","And then, feeling rather sick, he went to look for some parsley.":"99c0200cc9c44442a49a6d8549685366a6809aec32cdcb3742ed78399c1718e4","But round the end of a cucumber frame, whom should he meet but Mr. McGregor!":"338c4474c182a7fa449e74aa0b31a99ef6a1b1bfe670b000b8f62f9e829c9dc8","Mr. McGregor was on his hands and knees planting out young cabbages, but he jumped up and ran after Peter, waving a rake and calling out, “Stop thief!”":"588613bc83a2dd2c9cc5eaa94e1d0c1937783acf78550caf82a951ddce17e34a","Peter was most dreadfully frightened; he rushed all over the garden, for he had forgotten the way back to the gate. He lost one of his shoes among the cabbages, and the other shoe amongst the potatoes.":"a9a7a7031eca105b2d75ae435b5d96c84a6e0ea0afb347bbae8e95f32e93de9f","After losing them, he ran on four legs and went faster, so that I think he might have got away altogether if he had not unfortunately run into a gooseberry net, and got caught by the large buttons on his jacket. It was a blue jacket with brass buttons, quite new.":"3a49b213cb6966ad0c8b1beadbde4f7d89cd733fc1176c07fb7f1b1d7d313b9c","Peter gave himself up for lost, and shed big tears; but his sobs were overheard by some friendly sparrows, who flew to him in great excitement, and implored him to exert himself.":"fdaa82353806d7f97d8c13cbd1073ee3da8e8252e288eb1f42b1045983a9f691","Mr. McGregor came up with a sieve, which he intended to pop upon the top of Peter; but Peter wriggled out just in time, leaving his jacket behind him.":"0968a96c0bb0b46efa7f83f80aca9a392622c9759e9db6fdbbc50b5da2892c03","And rushed into the tool-shed, and jumped into a can. It would have been a beautiful thing to hide in, if it had not had so much water in it.":"0a62e42746a6f223345fc61240ac2f49e7ab42f3e0606db104b4a05f4c3f671d","Mr. McGregor was quite sure that Peter was somewhere in the tool-shed, perhaps hidden underneath a flower-pot. He began to turn them over carefully, looking under each. Presently Peter sneezed—“Kertyschoo!” Mr. McGregor was after him in no time.":"1e77dd98a17dbda7cdf72f4f07610d4063f2181283d2b8f2a9991df02848d713","And tried to put his foot upon Peter, who jumped out of a window, upsetting three plants. The window was too small for Mr. McGregor, and he was tired of running after Peter. He went back to his work.":"48b6bb6d90ec4cb69d574f08bd332ab8427c2647b1e9d3fba07f2bbd7b5615b3","Peter sat down to rest; he was out of breath and trembling with fright, and he had not the least idea which way to go. Also he was very damp with sitting in that can. After a time he began to wander about, going lippity—lippity—not very fast, and looking all round.":"1100a602463a9aed1d6e849772facef3010b87babb01c3cf0ca0d5f5f2cfd1bc","He found a door in a wall; but it was locked, and there was no room for a fat little rabbit to squeeze underneath. An old mouse was running in and out over the stone doorstep, carrying peas and beans to her family in the wood. Peter asked her the way to the gate, but she had such a large pea in her mouth that she could not answer. She only shook her head at him. Peter began to cry.":"38f70b5e1fe64b857ea5ca949b32e6be45c7e35c54aa7034dead0ab13fe10ee1","Then he tried to find his way straight across the garden, but he became more and more puzzled. Presently, he came to a pond where Mr. McGregor filled his water-cans. A white cat was staring at some gold-fish, she sat very, very still, but now and then the tip of her tail twitched as if it were alive. Peter thought it best to go away without speaking to her; he had heard about cats from his cousin, little Benjamin Bunny.":"d9c2ecdf029954c2fa603d25402c58760dc2356fe14a592e893d0c34f09876fd","He went back towards the tool-shed, but suddenly, quite close to him, he heard the noise of a hoe—scr-r-ritch, scratch, scratch, scritch. Peter scuttered underneath the bushes. But presently, as nothing happened, he came out, and climbed upon a wheelbarrow and peeped over. The first thing he saw was Mr. McGregor hoeing onions. His back was turned towards Peter, and beyond him was the gate!":"bbd4a1b08290a2ee0974839200104bd0fdbaea138069b36b273cceb503102aba","Peter got down very quietly off the wheelbarrow; and started running as fast as he could go, along a straight walk behind some black-currant bushes. Mr. McGregor caught sight of him at the corner, but Peter did not care. He slipped underneath the gate, and was safe at last in the wood outside the garden.":"0fb65b523b8cf1ddfbf4731e4b5ef4926bba4d9196490ece41b15465bbf32346","Mr. McGregor hung up the little jacket and the shoes for a scare-crow to frighten the blackbirds. Peter never stopped running or looked behind him till he got home to the big fir-tree.":"3040c0928e64c949d3f18d1c3f8fe0acd59f74683fc21fa4c491b10d3e374a7e","He was so tired that he flopped down upon the nice soft sand on the floor of the rabbit-hole and shut his eyes. His mother was busy cooking; she wondered what he had done with his clothes. It was the second little jacket and pair of shoes that Peter had lost in a fortnight!":"d1c11cc22067d301450d0247217e4842b8da2d211c4f4aac4aab3968bb2ab6a2","I am sorry to say that Peter was not very well during the evening. His mother put him to bed, and made some camomile tea; and she gave a dose of it to Peter! ‘One table-spoonful to be taken at bed-time.’":"9fc608077feeb4cfc2ead208c43f880616704e7c31626e54ea67ba606dedaef5","But Flopsy, Mopsy, and Cotton-tail had bread and milk and blackberries for supper. THE END":"2dee0774c6d7b52dd0ebba0f039451699105e0fbb5f17ac997ddf0c5d4e9bd98","Long ago, all the people on earth were one big family. They lived together and spoke only one language. Everyone could understand everyone else.":"a744f38e5754b4a813af9e328bad35934ec8f3c3a5f286583ac16943f4ccadf9","As the people travelled toward the east, they came to a wide, flat land called Shinar. “Let us stay here,” they said. And so this plain became their home.":"ea63f2ae80e0cee74e6660a87b6fb7eb614857e7ea44143bbc27e56473244c9d","“Come,” they said to one another, “let us make bricks and bake them hard in the fire.” They used bricks in the place of stone, and sticky tar to hold them together.":"c6d296423a5fce3f25fcd20d27d74e0a8d6d0f0c3e75d0c3984d679af34a1f10","Then the people dreamed a great dream. “Let us build a city, and a tower so tall that its very top reaches heaven! Then we will make a great name for ourselves, and we will never be scattered across the earth.”":"c1df7149b5dba67f513f0e683c54f00852a85d9a8d5f3895e659ae288f88c127","The tower grew higher and higher. Then the Lord came down to look at the city and the tower that the people were building. He saw that they were all one, with one language, and that nothing they planned to do would be too hard for them.":"3855708888982dc9f18b1222a3767e6f1a35f32e9d5958b7e00d31f71a44f2b8","So the Lord said, “Let us go down and mix up their language, so that they can no longer understand one another.” Suddenly the builders heard strange new words. No one could understand his friend!":"4b1eb897def20e7982a583869a7c898a1555cef548235f5d64b80a1fadad970e","The people could no longer work together, so they stopped building the city. They left the great tower unfinished, and spread out over the face of all the earth.":"a1dfe94722cf8244cccbb181c36a7fdffe8b8df4596e551b86c519eaca10a6d6","And that is why the city was called <b>Babel</b> — a name that sounds like the word for “confusion.” There the Lord mixed up the one language of the whole world, and from there its people spread across the earth.":"f1a8e2aa1f3ea44a41227cf2d8cbad405e4407177876a7c12393462e083c40d4","After Jesus had returned to heaven, his followers stayed together, praying and waiting as he had told them to. On the morning of a feast called Pentecost, they were all in one room, of one heart and one mind.":"d2be1679acd3ff2849a6b76b6e4e0374bc7c2d09d63aac106cd334b05670ce47","Suddenly, without any warning, a sound came from the sky — like a strong, rushing wind. It filled the whole house, roaring around them, though no window had been opened.":"517f83111e6cc7a1edfb2fd1b06169d04b83984925440169f277e52b6c0f5d42","Then something they had never seen appeared: little flames, shaped like tongues of fire, came to rest gently on the head of each one of them — glowing, but not burning.":"055e41d31533a2ada33db4e1d1183b071d626342fb912cca2f9bac06c99e4971","They were filled with God's Spirit, and they began to speak — but not in their own language. Each one found himself speaking in a language he had never learned, as the Spirit gave him the words.":"218bb49a047b4986dc8c8dfcfdd178b3b1c3fe7fdeec9614793633242016f6da","In Jerusalem that day were people from many faraway countries. A crowd gathered — and every single person was amazed, because each one could hear the friends speaking in his own home language.":"1f4d0722711ec853eac4c32dc9faf05516a522a960ae77976c9de8cd22ff5856","Long ago at Babel, one language became many, and the people were scattered apart. Now, with many languages, everyone understood — and the people were drawn together. What Babel had divided, this day began to heal.":"e45906bb9ba4e032ab2f2e94c01c0d5290e78cea0d994b8cfedbf2b9f8a3103b","On walls all over Egypt were rows of tiny pictures — birds, eyes, waves, little figures. Everyone could see them, but the meaning was lost. For fourteen hundred years, this was a language locked with no key.":"2a46585eba0cb2f8684dce55ddee6d4eecbf8f41ae9581a462cff2a599624695","In 1799, soldiers digging near a town called Rosetta struck something hard in the ground. It was a huge slab of dark stone, and every inch of one side was covered in careful rows of writing.":"81db440c37508b16df5441ad737b4aada0739825ea971b6f76b91d8c690bb25a","Scholars gathered round and saw something wonderful. The stone held the very same message written three times, in three different scripts — Egyptian hieroglyphs at the top, everyday Egyptian in the middle, and, at the bottom, Greek. And Greek, they could read!":"49b638da299431d7c3a09f27f57096e0c58dddad944c6d33f011a69d32a0b359","In France, a young man named Jean-François Champollion had loved languages since he was a boy. He taught himself script after script — Latin, Greek, Hebrew, Arabic, and more — dreaming that one day he would read the hieroglyphs no one else could.":"244c8f21db78dec745427dd87ab89bf042dbe27e5b93b5347cfb5482702f6317","Champollion noticed that some names — kings and queens — were drawn inside little ovals. He guessed that these signs might stand for <b>sounds</b>, like letters. Matching “Ptolemy” and “Cleopatra” sign by sign, sound by sound, the hieroglyphs slowly began to speak.":"ef4d9e18b79fe24ab2fed0a07dda4b83a3516b28c87935862f3ed917ad642608","One day in 1822, it all came together. The story says Champollion ran to his brother, cried out that he had done it, and fainted from the shock of joy. Egypt's lost language could be read again — and three thousand years of stories swung open like a door.":"b8ef654a5f5aa86a14cec8b2dd468b2c6deb58008671df12035c60068f6ac681","Helen could not see the faces of the people she loved, and could not hear their voices. She had thoughts and feelings, but no way to share them. When no one could understand her, she often grew angry and afraid, lost in the silent dark.":"794f3d445ab169de7d654768a155724a53f5c4ab0941be989e3efd74e4900dca","Then a teacher named Anne Sullivan came to live with her. Anne would take Helen's hand and, with quick finger movements, spell words into her palm — d-o-l-l, when she gave her a doll. Helen felt the shapes, but did not know they meant anything.":"68063ad736453b08cfee0bd2abf4a174aa55c00e2909e850e553e0c698909fb3","Helen learned to copy the finger-letters back, quick and clever, the way you might copy a game with your hands. But it was only a game to her. She still did not understand the great secret: that every thing in the world has a name.":"897f9e9524531c0c896095040fb1494827606b263adf567187e0ae955e50a4d1","One warm morning, Anne led Helen out to the well-house, where someone was pumping water. She held Helen's hand under the spout. As the cool water rushed over it, she spelled into the other hand the word <span>w-a-t-e-r</span> — first slowly, then again and again.":"775c040d992a4852d9a6938023f653c2ab75d6b285ad8a99b9345abb5d161c16","Suddenly, like a flash of light, Helen understood. The cool something rushing over her hand and the word spelled into her palm were the same thing — this was <span>water</span>! In that instant the secret of language opened, and her whole mind woke up.":"d100031285d674a17274fdfb1e37595fbc1112d5533ee6aef99f572a697b18b3","That day Helen learned the names of everything she touched, and the whole world seemed new. Words became her door out of the dark. She would grow up to read, write, go to college, and speak to people everywhere — all beginning with one word poured into her hand.":"7fec8b3441a14d4e1e3e01c83bf2ee116118893c04c1849ca098b49e39fe2973","An Ox came down to a reedy pool to drink. As he splashed heavily into the water, he crushed a young Frog into the mud.":"9b05e91b7952fa4d7d15b100d9ce533c1fbd1a18ccfafc09b0556774ce2556cd","The old Frog soon missed the little one and asked his brothers and sisters what had become of him. “A great big monster,” said one of them, “stepped on little brother with one of his huge feet!”":"582a5d31f81a7515b23029f42e8dab99781176ab8ab46edb89914e2a0860c7da","“Big, was he!” said the old Frog, puffing herself up. “Was he as big as this?” “Oh, much bigger!” they cried. The Frog puffed up still more. “He could not have been bigger than this,” she said. But the little Frogs all declared that the monster was much, much bigger and the old Frog kept puffing herself out more and more until, all at once, she burst.":"a7772ff45dd10617bf884e2d4b01f788cc84f82f1f46f8d30e7c574667bcb09e","The Mice once called a meeting to decide on a plan to free themselves of their enemy, the Cat. At least they wished to find some way of knowing when she was coming, so they might have time to run away. Indeed, something had to be done, for they lived in such constant fear of her claws that they hardly dared stir from their dens by night or day.":"f02774a88b8cca3ef1f94d868f8e2e6a9433557c8a09c3c14b9fc280616fa814","Many plans were discussed, but none of them was thought good enough. At last a very young Mouse got up and said: “I have a plan that seems very simple, but I know it will be successful. All we have to do is to hang a bell about the Cat’s neck. When we hear the bell ringing we will know immediately that our enemy is coming.”":"6e93323d6faec615b579936f683e09172415b8196292e0563c86a0d8548d7b31","All the Mice were much surprised that they had not thought of such a plan before. But in the midst of the rejoicing over their good fortune, an old Mouse arose and said: “I will say that the plan of the young Mouse is very good. But let me ask one question: Who will bell the Cat?”":"d1a56a5ff4395e01e8ff8347626adddd6b479b9df04df407b592431741c5c5f1","A Fox one day spied a beautiful bunch of ripe grapes hanging from a vine trained along the branches of a tree. The grapes seemed ready to burst with juice, and the Fox’s mouth watered as he gazed longingly at them. The bunch hung from a high branch, and the Fox had to jump for it. The first time he jumped he missed it by a long way. So he walked off a short distance and took a running leap at it, only to fall short once more. Again and again he tried, but in vain.":"eda9ae58d6190e30ef0c9f4ccba17b36aaee8da1ccf816c691ac95e7f48ad39e","Now he sat down and looked at the grapes in disgust. “What a fool I am,” he said. “Here I am wearing myself out to get a bunch of sour grapes that are not worth gaping for.” And off he walked very, very scornfully.":"e84477f46ca64198eea03eaae0c8e9899975c67febdce971210bbf79a3c0620f","A Lion lay asleep in the forest, his great head resting on his paws. A timid little Mouse came upon him unexpectedly, and in her fright and haste to get away, ran across the Lion’s nose. Roused from his nap, the Lion laid his huge paw angrily on the tiny creature to kill her.":"521995ee671bdd94a97eab38b3356b730e314d9d10a57a531fb54ffac9b25379","“Spare me!” begged the poor Mouse. “Please let me go and some day I will surely repay you.” The Lion was much amused to think that a Mouse could ever help him. But he was generous and finally let the Mouse go.":"04b80bef50a4381d4897b96baad835cfbb6eee722b416b817c6df07df067d703","Some days later, while stalking his prey in the forest, the Lion was caught in the toils of a hunter’s net. Unable to free himself, he filled the forest with his angry roaring. The Mouse knew the voice and quickly found the Lion struggling in the net. Running to one of the great ropes that bound him, she gnawed it until it parted, and soon the Lion was free. “You laughed when I said I would repay you,” said the Mouse. “Now you see that even a Mouse can help a Lion.”":"478a475dc69adeb3a77be5c8384d38caa809d1cb4a5885b27f846f8f7961d874","In a spell of dry weather, when the Birds could find very little to drink, a thirsty Crow found a pitcher with a little water in it. But the pitcher was high and had a narrow neck, and no matter how he tried, the Crow could not reach the water. The poor thing felt as if he must die of thirst.":"5a695dc5497a886ad5f394bc6d9d7c506abe3a49a7d139ae5a74dd4283fe33ac","Then an idea came to him. Picking up some small pebbles, he dropped them into the pitcher one by one. With each pebble the water rose a little higher until at last it was near enough so he could drink.":"2ef5f8752c8311fc3632870c99c769d143fb3dd23964e8ff513440f24b9a5978","A Hare was making fun of the Tortoise one day for being so slow. “Do you ever get anywhere?” he asked with a mocking laugh. “Yes,” replied the Tortoise, “and I get there sooner than you think. I’ll run you a race and prove it.”":"01a5c58ef84e963d81bfd76b6bce6cd770d40e9a469bec32ed1a7240184d8702","The Hare was much amused at the idea of running a race with the Tortoise, but for the fun of the thing he agreed. So the Fox, who had consented to act as judge, marked the distance and started the runners off. The Hare was soon far out of sight, and to make the Tortoise feel very deeply how ridiculous it was for him to try a race with a Hare, he lay down beside the course to take a nap until the Tortoise should catch up.":"18bd109bd3f0629f2a5e41bcf1c8663ed28f346f1ef9a410234bfa5b21a393b2","The Tortoise meanwhile kept going slowly but steadily, and, after a time, passed the place where the Hare was sleeping. But the Hare slept on very peacefully; and when at last he did wake up, the Tortoise was near the goal. The Hare now ran his swiftest, but he could not overtake the Tortoise in time.":"d6a7a7fb402ac2a4b1686ef733a09402d0251c9803322af9fa34aa6785998e45","One bright morning as the Fox was following his sharp nose through the wood in search of a bite to eat, he saw a Crow on the limb of a tree overhead. This was by no means the first Crow the Fox had ever seen. What caught his attention this time and made him stop for a second look, was that the lucky Crow held a bit of cheese in her beak. “No need to search any farther,” thought sly Master Fox. “Here is a dainty bite for my breakfast.”":"0c32a9c33310d3c94720f364b8f7c5469869a7814f735aa7b8e9fd5459fd325a","Up he trotted to the foot of the tree in which the Crow was sitting, and looking up admiringly, he cried, “Good-morning, beautiful creature!” The Crow, her head cocked on one side, watched the Fox suspiciously. But she kept her beak tightly closed on the cheese and did not return his greeting. “What a charming creature she is!” said the Fox. “How her feathers shine! What a beautiful form and what splendid wings! Such a wonderful Bird should have a very lovely voice, since everything else about her is so perfect. Could she sing just one song, I know I should hail her Queen of Birds.”":"9eba19c87cb28f208ef8262bad1b9228ee003eda1be34256d1c6e82c18a2a085","Listening to these flattering words, the Crow forgot all her suspicion, and also her breakfast. She wanted very much to be called Queen of Birds. So she opened her beak wide to utter her loudest caw, and down fell the cheese straight into the Fox’s open mouth. “Thank you,” said Master Fox sweetly, as he walked off. “Though it is cracked, you have a voice sure enough. But where are your wits?”":"28ba882aee1c0e403aea4cc15109860efe6e5c5cd14c77a5d221597ee4b06a7d","A certain Father had a family of Sons, who were forever quarreling among themselves. No words he could say did the least good, so he cast about in his mind for some very striking example that should make them see that discord would lead them to misfortune. One day when the quarreling had been much more violent than usual and each of the Sons was moping in a surly manner, he asked one of them to bring him a bundle of sticks. Then handing the bundle to each of his Sons in turn he told them to try to break it. But although each one tried his best, none was able to do so.":"ef5debbd6540c7709a33cce2f9d51061c72c5720b72b2893df5dc69d6da8f0d4","The Father then untied the bundle and gave the sticks to his Sons to break one by one. This they did very easily. “My Sons,” said the Father, “do you not see how certain it is that if you agree with each other and help each other, it will be impossible for your enemies to injure you? But if you are divided among yourselves, you will be no stronger than a single stick in that bundle.”":"8173a380d45507f6eea2b66b23755aa62b777ac4fab7979bdd10eec3a1b49af5","A Shepherd Boy tended his master’s Sheep near a dark forest not far from the village. Soon he found life in the pasture very dull. All he could do to amuse himself was to talk to his dog or play on his shepherd’s pipe. One day as he sat watching the Sheep and the quiet forest, and thinking what he would do should he see a Wolf, he thought of a plan to amuse himself.":"9faa90a6a01d55806f7cbd808f865bbc9114863043a0c12c5fd08403ccec2459","His Master had told him to call for help should a Wolf attack the flock, and the Villagers would drive it away. So now, though he had not seen anything that even looked like a Wolf, he ran toward the village shouting at the top of his voice, “Wolf! Wolf!” As he expected, the Villagers who heard the cry dropped their work and ran in great excitement to the pasture. But when they got there they found the Boy doubled up with laughter at the trick he had played on them.":"6b40819992f40b548da7dd2def8c23f970d8cbd21aaeca6cf0a8c603e1f15a27","A few days later the Shepherd Boy again shouted, “Wolf! Wolf!” Again the Villagers ran to help him, only to be laughed at again. Then one evening as the sun was setting behind the forest and the shadows were creeping out over the pasture, a Wolf really did spring from the underbrush and fall upon the Sheep. In terror the Boy ran toward the village shouting “Wolf! Wolf!” But though the Villagers heard the cry, they did not run to help him as they had before. “He cannot fool us again,” they said. The Wolf killed a great many of the Boy’s sheep and then slipped away into the forest.":"59730675f954d308d88c5daad3c70d2d350f1e99ce8cddaf87d6e7da5a835cfc","There was once a Countryman who possessed the most wonderful Goose you can imagine, for every day when he visited the nest, the Goose had laid a beautiful, glittering, golden egg. The Countryman took the eggs to market and soon began to get rich. But it was not long before he grew impatient with the Goose because she gave him only a single golden egg a day. He was not getting rich fast enough.":"1f3c13a6929a5b74e8a039d0fb392bd0e5184f20fdcc9f3166fbb29b0415ce8a","Then one day, after he had finished counting his money, the idea came to him that he could get all the golden eggs at once by killing the Goose and cutting it open. But when the deed was done, not a single golden egg did he find, and his precious Goose was dead.":"414c6fe59b7edc1f7a6f750a72aa2f0d335d9a0d5d329f0f80b21410c2c4284e","A Milkmaid had been out to milk the cows and was returning from the field with the shining milk pail balanced nicely on her head. As she walked along, her pretty head was busy with plans for the days to come. “This good, rich milk,” she mused, “will give me plenty of cream to churn. The butter I make I will take to market, and with the money I get for it I will buy a lot of eggs for hatching. How nice it will be when they are all hatched and the yard is full of fine young chicks. Then when May day comes I will sell them, and with the money I’ll buy a lovely new dress to wear to the fair. All the young men will look at me. They will come and try to make love to me,—but I shall very quickly send them about their business!”":"b379d6a75d7dbe673a93e73d3e3a5e97be87e9fe1d2312bed9139e7ac5866110","As she thought of how she would settle that matter, she tossed her head scornfully, and down fell the pail of milk to the ground. And all the milk flowed out, and with it vanished butter and eggs and chicks and new dress and all the milkmaid’s pride.":"60220cc8badd7f4c647a33f31175cd731f4b0a345e7a291d149b83860a685df0","The North Wind and the Sun had a quarrel about which of them was the stronger. While they were disputing with much heat and bluster, a Traveler passed along the road wrapped in a cloak. “Let us agree,” said the Sun, “that he is the stronger who can strip that Traveler of his cloak.” “Very well,” growled the North Wind, and at once sent a cold, howling blast against the Traveler.":"d5f6595b8157e70bacddcb1e341864edc7646011bdd554f9afade24bbeb52cde","With the first gust of wind the ends of the cloak whipped about the Traveler’s body. But he immediately wrapped it closely around him, and the harder the Wind blew, the tighter he held it to him. The North Wind tore angrily at the cloak, but all his efforts were in vain.":"4de95fa0275530b8c1f29ceee88ca6aeb5c075dd35edaa550328d6b89bc85d73","Then the Sun began to shine. At first his beams were gentle, and in the pleasant warmth after the bitter cold of the North Wind, the Traveler unfastened his cloak and let it hang loosely from his shoulders. The Sun’s rays grew warmer and warmer. The man took off his cap and mopped his brow. At last he became so heated that he pulled off his cloak, and, to escape the blazing sunshine, threw himself down in the welcome shade of a tree by the roadside.":"d6d893d180ac3fad4238878bd508fb28849c43e1870c747e9b1bd0ba4aa439c5"}